The lifetime module has four CPU-bound readers, RCU read sections, a static
published object and 1024 enable/disable/grace-period/clear cycles. It never
frees or dereferences that object: a missing pointer is counted, not crashed.
No BPF, hotplug, scheduler callback or debugger stop is needed to observe this
failure. memory_gate=1 replaces branch patching with a release/acquire Boolean.
The same build/payload runs each mode on each kernel.

Final fixed TCG Cortex-A72, four CPUs, 2048 MiB, same 64 MiB snapshot disk:
                    static-key reads / missing       memory-gate reads / missing
GCC reference       130167215 / 10068125 FAIL           51990477 / 0 PASS
EvoCC subject        69865759 /  5043611 FAIL           33672713 / 0 PASS
The first missing observation on every reader sees instruction d503201f (NOP)
and key count 0. This after-the-fact observation alone does NOT establish
when the read section entered, stale translation, or a broken grace period.
Both kernels boot and power off; the existing TAP parser separately records
the static-key test exit 1 and memory control exit 0. Boot PASS is not test PASS.

The independent static-key-immediate.c control has no RCU or object publication.
First, 256 toggles check the calling CPU and synchronous callbacks on all CPUs.
Then four concurrent readers check 512 toggles: an odd sequence excludes an
update, acquire plus a full barrier and matching even sequences delimit a
stable-state query. The expected Boolean is derived from that sequence.
Final GCC: 28441826 stable queries, zero mismatches.
Final Evo: 23169747 stable queries, zero mismatches.
Both have zero immediate/remote mismatches; real module load/unload and TAP
1/1, exit 0 pass. These positive controls narrow, but do not close, K01.

Earlier bounded variants, each with its own retained payload/serial identity:
  Disable TCG TB chaining only: GCC 1687725 missing, Evo 796471 missing; FAIL.
  KEY_READER_ISB before the branch: GCC 12867988, Evo 3562129 missing; FAIL.
  KEY_READER_MB before the branch: GCC 5954592, Evo 3498941 missing; FAIL.
Those are diagnostic changes, not production repairs or accepted lane changes.
The initial uninstrumented payload passed on HVF on both kernels, but differs
from the final source. Neither that result nor the simpler controls promotes
the unchanged sched_ext hotplug/reload sequence to PASS.

Build environment: evo-flight-lab:sealed, linux/arm64, network none,
GCC 14.2.0-19. Diagnostic modules are GCC-built infrastructure on BOTH kernels;
this is not additional EvoCC compilation credit. Source and kernel build roots
are read-only; only the small external-module directory is writable.
  make -s -C /work/src O=/work/kernel ARCH=arm64 CC=gcc-14 HOSTCC=gcc-14
    M=/work/probe/driver -j2 modules
  scripts/sign-file sha256 <existing matching key/certificate> <diagnostic.ko>
  gcc-14 -static -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror <loader.c> -o <loader>
  usr/gen_init_cpio -t 0 <payload.spec> > <payload.cpio>
The two preprocessor controls use KCFLAGS=-DKEY_READER_ISB or
-DKEY_READER_MB; final baseline explicitly resets KCFLAGS=.
Small make clock-skew warnings (under 0.1 seconds) were retained; new payloads
were produced and actually executed. No full kernel was rebuilt or replaced.

